well let's continue to talk about  tonight's meaningful vote all the  build-up to a we've got a former press  secretary Dominick rabe with us now good  morning to you yeah very good thank you  and it's an interesting day it's  exciting it's frightening for some  people a lot of people nerves about  what's going to happen and the Prime  Minister's been issuing rallying  speeches and cries ahead of this and  she's basically been saying and that no  brexit is now more likely than No Deal  if MPs don't vote her plans through she  worked to do that I'm not going to  question her  tactics for trying to whip up support  for her deal that's quite right and  quite a proper no I don't think it's  right and in law because of the EU  withdrawal Act we'll leave on the 29th  of March unless there's legislation to  change it and that can't happen unless  the Prime Minister change her mind and  that was confirmed over the weekend by  the former president the Supreme Court  Lord Neuberger in an interview and it's  interesting because I'm just looking at  the possible outcomes for this vote  tonight and people are saying that these  plans are a move towards a softer brexit  along the lines of a Norway model and  why do you think that would be a bad  thing well if you look at Norway in the  model we lose control of something like  75 percent of our laws we don't take  control of our immigration at all that's  one of the supposed answers of this deal  as bad as it is and we wouldn't have an  independent free trade policy so I don't  think that that's going to be the right  answer for the country and it's not  clear that those countries in the EEA  would want us anyway that deal is  bespoke for them we're much bigger  economy we've got different needs  cabinet meeting this morning at around  9:30 what you want your breaks to tear  colleagues to be saying to the prime  minister at the moment because these are  the final moments before MPs head in to  vote the stakes are high second guess  what the cabinet individually will give  as advice the Prime Minister well I  would you like to say if you were still  in there what would you be saying this  morning I'd continue to give the advice  I faithfully given which is that we need  to be delivering brexit faithful to the  referenda which means taking back  control of our borders are those no  money I wouldn't throw our hands up in  the air and despair when this deal is I  expect gets voted down we should  continue to extend the arm a friend  to our new partners we want to continue  negotiations well I think we always want  to see cross-party approach but the one  thing above all is we're leaving the EU  in March of next year we can't allow  anyone to prostrate or veto that from  happening that was the mandate we had  biggest Democratic mandate in history we  must deliver on those promises okay  we've got some questions from viewers at  this door for you ere we opened up  Twitter let's take a look at some words  this is from Sergey 20 asks is a hard  Irish border an inevitable consequence  of a No Deal if not why not if there is  no border how do you prevent movement  across it lot of people we'd make clear  that we would not see a return to a hard  border the head of HMRC does the customs  said that we could continue to operate  with our Irish partners on on the South  without any need for any infrastructure  at the border the Irish have said the  same  so trust the experts trust what both the  UK and the Republic have said and I  don't think that in the spirit of unity  Brussels when push came to shove we'll  want to see would want to push in and  see a hard border bet it'll be appalling  for European unity and I don't think  even with all the darker swirling  Machiavellian forces in Brussels they're  actually when push comes to shove go to  force that on the Republic we would not  do it here okay and we put this question  to scarce armored actually and we'll put  it to you as well this Robin John  pillock never mind the MPs confidence in  the Prime Minister what about the UK  citizens confidence with Parliament now  that's the that's the general consensus  I'm getting on Twitter this morning is  that the people at home are thinking our  MPs going in there with my best  interests at heart today or are they  voting in line with a party in line with  their own interests how can you restore  confidence well look in terms of party  the divisions within the party show that  people aren't just going for tribal  loyalties but I think the number one  thing is that we had a referendum big  it's democratic mandate in history that  we've ever seen in our lifetimes and we  need to fulfil the promises the number  one thing that frustrates people is when  you don't keep your promises both labour  and the Conservatives went into the last  election saying we would give effect to  the referendum we would  leave the EU and we would leave the  single market we've got to be true to  those promises we've all made and this  is from Michael Riley when war both  labour and the Conservatives pull  together and deliver on the vote they  the vote was to leave as you quite  rightly pointed out if a vote was to  remain  I for one would accept it and move on  that's what a lot of people can't  understand is there's so much bickering  going on in there that has held up the  process there's been you know road bumps  the entire way why can't the two sides  come together to deliver on this and  then we can all get on with you know the  matter domestic matters I would love to  live in the political land of milk and  honey where there was consensus but this  has been a very divisive vote for every  political party and for the country at  large but you're absolutely right to say  that we've got to try and come together  and that's why I think the efforts to  try and derail or scupper brexit will be  so damaging to public trust in our  political system

